<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Orlande de Lassus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1532–<a href="/years/1594">1594</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Orlando di Lasso</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Orlande de Lassus was a Franco-Flemish composer, born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mons">Mons</a> in Hainaut around 1532, and one of the most prolific and celebrated musicians of the late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a>. From 1556 he served the Wittelsbach court of Albert V in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a>, leading its chapel from 1563 until his death in 1594. He wrote more than 2,000 works, including masses, motets, madrigals and chansons.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/orlande-de-lassus.jpg" alt="Orlande de Lassus" width="320" height="304" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Orlande_de_Lassus.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The county also produced leading artists: the composer <b>Orlande de Lassus</b> was born in Mons around 1532, and the painter Antoine Watteau in Valenciennes in 1684, shortly after its cession to France.</blockquote><a href="/empire/county-of-hainaut">County of Hainaut · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>In the 16th century the Munich court of Albert V employed the composer <b>Orlande de Lassus</b> and built collections that grew into the Bavarian State Library.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-bavaria">Duchy of Bavaria · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Mons-en-Laonnois, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=49.54,3.55&amp;year=1572" aria-label="Mons-en-Laonnois on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4632 438 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Orlande de Lassus"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5082 708h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5082 708h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=49.54,3.55&amp;year=1572">What was here in 1572? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
